<p>The Norwegian method made an uncomfortable idea fashionable: that training properly means measuring your lactate. Double thresholds, test strips, finger pricks between reps. It's what Ingebrigtsen and company do, and it works — for them.</p>

<p>For you, almost certainly, it isn't necessary. Not because lactate is useless, but because <strong>the number you'll actually act on comes out just as well from a properly executed field test</strong>. For a runner training 6 to 12 hours a week, the gap between measuring lactate and not measuring it is far smaller than the gap between testing well and testing badly.</p>

<p>This is the whole path: the test, the zones that come out of it, and the two sessions that genuinely move threshold.</p>

<h2>What threshold is, in one sentence</h2>

<p>It's the highest intensity you can hold before the effort starts getting away from you — roughly an hour flat out, for most people. Above that point your body produces more lactate than it can clear, and the clock starts running against you.</p>

<p>Strips give you that point with laboratory precision. A field test gives it to you with sufficient precision. And since <em>every</em> one of your zones is calculated from it, sufficient is exactly what you need.</p>

<h2>The test: 30 minutes flat out</h2>

<p>One test, and this is it:</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>Run 30 minutes at the hardest effort you can sustain.</strong> The pace isn't prescribed — you go at whatever you can hold.</p>
  <p><strong>Threshold pace</strong> = your average pace across the full 30 minutes.<br>
     <strong>LTHR</strong> (lactate threshold heart rate) = your average heart rate over the <em>last 20</em> minutes.</p>
</div>

<p>The first 10 minutes get discarded for heart rate because your pulse is still climbing toward its plateau. Pace is averaged across the whole thing.</p>

<p>This is the only point in the method where feel overrides the number. Every other session gives you a prescribed target; here we're measuring where that target actually is.</p>

<h3>How to execute it, which is where nearly everyone ruins it</h3>

<p>A badly executed test doesn't give you a poor number. It gives you a false one, and you'll train off it for the next two or three months. That's worse than not testing.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Early, cool, rested, fed and hydrated.</strong> Not at the end of a hard week.</li>
  <li><strong>Flat, uninterrupted route.</strong> No traffic lights. If you stop, the test is void.</li>
  <li><strong>Chest strap, mandatory.</strong> Wrist optical sensors are least accurate in exactly the range we're measuring.</li>
  <li><strong>If it goes wrong, diagnose and repeat the following weekend.</strong> The two usual causes are GI trouble and pacing it badly. Fix them with a low-fibre day before, white carbs at breakfast, and coffee timed properly.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you had to stop, you can salvage something by calculating off the last 10 clean minutes. But that's a fallback, not a result.</p>

<p><strong>A race works too.</strong> Any genuinely all-out race gets cross-checked against threshold calculations. If you've just raced a 10K hard, you already have the data — don't add a test to tired legs.</p>

