<h3>Zone X: the marathon-pace trap</h3>

<p>Zone X is the upper half of zone 2 — roughly your marathon pace. We separate it from the rest of Z2 for a concrete reason: <strong>it accumulates considerably more fatigue than low zone 2 without delivering much more aerobic benefit in return</strong>.</p>

<p>It's the zone where most people quietly over-train. You head out for an "easy run", you feel good, the pace settles a few seconds quicker on its own, and you end up spending three sessions a week in X. It feels like training. Then you arrive at your quality day with no legs.</p>

<p>If your week has two hard sessions, everything else goes in low Z2. Not in X.</p>

<h3>Zone Y: neither one thing nor the other</h3>

<p>Zone Y is the band right around threshold, the lower part of what most plans call zone 4. We separate it because <strong>the real benefits of zone 4 show up higher</strong>, and because training in Y feels hard without quite being hard.</p>

<p>Y isn't forbidden — it's where transitions and the tail end of some tempo runs naturally land. But it isn't where you program a whole session. If you're going to pay the cost of a hard session, pay it above Y.</p>

<h2>The two sessions that move threshold</h2>

<p>Everything above exists so these two can be prescribed properly. A typical threshold-building week has one of each, with low Z2 in between.</p>

<h3>Extensive: 30 minutes continuous at 95–100%</h3>

<p>Continuous, no breaks, just under or right at threshold. It's the session that shifts threshold upward the most, and the easiest to ruin: go out at 105% because you feel good and it falls apart at 18 minutes, and the session loses its point.</p>

<p>Progression across a block is <strong>lengthen before you accelerate</strong>: 20' → 25' → 30' → 2×20' at the same intensity. Only once 30 minutes sits comfortably do you raise the pace, and that normally means it's time to re-test.</p>

<h3>Intensive: 3 × 15 minutes at 100–103%</h3>

<p>With 2 to 3 minutes of easy jogging between reps. At threshold or just above, in chunks you can hold with good form.</p>

<p>One clarification that matters: <strong>the higher the percentage, the shorter the rep has to be</strong>. 3×15 works at 100–103%. If you want to work at 105%, the session is 5×8 minutes, not 3×15 — at that intensity, 45 minutes of work isn't sustainable for anyone who doesn't do this for a living.</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>The percentages are of speed, not of pace in min/km or min/mile.</strong> 103% of threshold means 3% <em>faster</em>. If your threshold is 4:00/km (6:26/mi), 103% is about 3:53/km (6:15/mi) — not 4:07.</p>
</div>

<h2>How often to re-test</h2>

<p>Every 2 to 3 months if you're training consistently. Two signals to bring it forward:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Perceived effort dropped</strong> at the prescribed paces, and decoupling between heart rate and pace is low or negative. Translation: you improved and your zones are stale.</li>
  <li><strong>You're back from an interruption</strong> — illness, travel, irregular weeks. Two comeback weeks first (easy Z1–Z2 plus activations), then test. Expect a lower threshold. That's normal, and adjusting now saves you six weeks of training above where you actually are.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Coach's note</h2>

<p>With every athlete who starts, I prescribe a threshold test per sport in the first few weeks. If they were already active, we test in week 1. If not, we do two weeks of base and activation and test after that — testing someone detrained just produces a number that'll be obsolete within a month.</p>

<p>That first test sets the baseline. From there everything becomes verifiable: <strong>if your threshold is faster than three months ago, you improved. If you're holding a higher threshold heart rate, you improved. If the same pace now costs you fewer beats, you improved.</strong> That isn't a feeling or an opinion — it's the same test, repeated, with numbers you can compare.</p>

<p>That's what makes coaching data-driven rather than a motivational chat: we don't debate whether you're better, we measure it. And when the number doesn't move, we change the plan instead of asking you to try harder.</p>

